=== Dalīl of Imam al-Shāfiʿī
<dalil-of-imam-al-shafii>
#quote(block: true)[
اَلطَّوَافُ بِالْبَيْتِ صَلَاةٌ

#emph["Ṭawāf of the House is (like) ṣalāh."]

#strong[Ref:] Hadith --- as cited in class (narrated from Ibn ʿAbbās رضي
الله عنه; reported in al-Tirmidhī, al-Nasā'ī, and others --- verify
exact numbering).
]

#strong[Imam al-Shāfiʿī's reasoning:] The Prophet ﷺ equated ṭawāf with
ṣalāh #strong[absolutely]. Since ṭahārah is a #strong[shart] of ṣalāh
(ṣalāh is #strong[invalid] without it), ṭahārah must equally be a
#strong[shart] of ṭawāf --- making it #strong[farḍ].

=== Ḥanafī response --- the Tashbīh (resemblance) argument
<hanafi-response-the-tashbih-resemblance-argument>
The Ḥanafī position rests on two pillars:

#strong[Pillar 1 --- The Qur'anic command is absolute (muṭlaq):]

#quote(block: true)[
وَلْيَطَّوَّفُوا بِالْبَيْتِ الْعَتِيقِ

#emph["And let them circumambulate the Ancient House."]

#strong[Ref:] Surah al-Ḥajj, 22:29.
]

- The Qur'an commands ṭawāf #strong[absolutely] --- with #strong[no
  mention of ṭahārah].
- Any #strong[addition] (like ṭahārah as a condition) that comes from
  #strong[ḥadīth] cannot raise the ruling to #strong[farḍ]\; it can
  #strong[maximally] establish #strong[wujūb].
- This is because the ḥadīth in question is a #strong[khabar wāḥid (خبر
  واحد)] \[solitary report\], which --- in Ḥanafī uṣūl --- cannot
  override or add to the #strong[qaṭʿī] (definitive) Qur'anic text at
  the level of #strong[farḍiyyah].

#strong[Pillar 2 --- Tashbīh does not demand complete similarity:]

The word #strong["ṣalāh"] in the ḥadīth is a #strong[tashbīh (تشبيه)]
\[simile/resemblance\], not an equation of identity. When something is
compared to another:

- Similarity is required in #strong[some] aspects, #strong[not all].
- #strong[Example from the Qur'an:] The wives of the Prophet ﷺ are
  called "mothers of the believers" --- but this does not mean they are
  biological mothers in #strong[every] respect (e.g.~inheritance does
  not apply).
- #strong[Application:] Ṭawāf is #strong[like] ṣalāh in that
  #strong[some kind of obligation] attaches --- but the #strong[degree]
  of obligation differs:
  - In #strong[ṣalāh]: ṭahārah is a #strong[shart (farḍ)] --- ṣalāh is
    #strong[invalid] without it.
  - In #strong[ṭawāf]: ṭahārah is #strong[wājib] --- ṭawāf is valid but
    defective without it.

#strong[Conclusion:] The Ḥanafī school practises upon #strong[both] the
āyah and the ḥadīth --- the Qur'an keeps ṭawāf #strong[absolute] (no
farḍ status for ṭahārah), and the ḥadīth raises ṭahārah to the level of
#strong[wājib].

