=== Wājib 1 --- Walking on the feet (المشي على الأقدام)
<wajib-1-walking-on-the-feet>
#strong[Ruling (Ḥanafī):] It is #strong[wājib] to perform ṭawāf
#strong[walking on one's feet] --- using a wheelchair, riding an animal,
or being carried is #strong[not permissible] unless there is a genuine
#strong[ʿudhr (عذر)] \[excuse/disability\].

#strong[Ruling (Shāfiʿī):] Walking on the feet is #strong[not wājib] ---
riding or using a wheelchair is permissible without any penalty.

==== Dalīl (Ḥanafī)
<dalil-hanafi>
The linguistic meaning of #strong[ṭawāf (طواف)] and specifically the
Qur'anic command:

#quote(block: true)[
وَلْيَطَّوَّفُوا بِالْبَيْتِ الْعَتِيقِ

#strong[Ref:] Surah al-Ḥajj, 22:29.
]

- The Arabic verb for ṭawāf linguistically implies #strong[circling
  while walking]. A person who rides is not called #strong[ṭā'if (طائف)]
  in the linguistic sense.
- Therefore, walking on feet is inherent to the meaning of ṭawāf itself.

==== Dalīl (Shāfiʿī) --- the Prophet's practice
<dalil-shafii-the-prophets-practice>
The Prophet ﷺ performed ṭawāf #strong[while riding] upon his animal
during the Farewell Hajj.

==== Ḥanafī explanation of the Prophet's riding
<hanafi-explanation-of-the-prophets-riding>
Two explanations were given:

+ #strong[He was older and heavier] --- as explained by #strong[Ibn
  ʿAbbās رضي الله عنه] --- so this was a concession due to ʿudhr.
+ #strong[For taʿlīm (تعليم)] \[teaching\] --- he rode so the people
  could #strong[see him] above the crowd and #strong[learn] the actions
  and sequence of ṭawāf from him.

