==== Special case: Crawling (الحبو)
<special-case-crawling>
- #strong[Crawling] (like a baby) is #strong[not] considered walking on
  the feet.
- If a person is #strong[capable] of walking but crawls, he is
  #strong[missing the wājib] → same ruling as riding.
- If he crawls because of #strong[ʿudhr] → concession applies, no
  penalty.

==== Nadhr (vow) to perform ṭawāf while crawling
<nadhr-vow-to-perform-awaf-while-crawling>
If someone vowed: #emph["I will perform ṭawāf for Allāh while
crawling"]:

- The #strong[ṭawāf] itself becomes #strong[wājib] because of the nadhr.
- The #strong[condition] of crawling is #strong[invalid] --- it is an
  improper condition and is dropped.
- He must perform the ṭawāf #strong[walking normally] on his feet.
- #strong[Preferred opinion (rājiḥ):] The crawling condition is voided,
  and the ṭawāf must be performed properly.
- #strong[Other opinion:] Crawling could suffice --- based on the
  analogy that if someone vows ṣalāh on unlawfully seized land, the
  ṣalāh is still valid despite the improper circumstance. This opinion
  is #strong[not the preferred one].

==== Being carried by another person
<being-carried-by-another-person>
If a person is #strong[carried] (on shoulders, wheelchair, etc.) while
#strong[capable of walking]: he is #strong[missing the wājib]. Same
ruling --- repeat if in Makkah; dam (goat) if returned home.

#horizontalrule

=== Wājib 2 --- Starting from Ḥajar al-Aswad (الابتداء من الحجر الأسود)
<wajib-2-starting-from-hajar-al-aswad>
#strong[Ruling (Ḥanafī preferred opinion): Sunnah] --- not farḍ or
wājib.

- The Prophet ﷺ started his ṭawāf from #strong[Ḥajar al-Aswad] --- this
  is the established sunnah.
- If someone starts from #strong[another point], his ṭawāf is
  #strong[valid] but he earns the #strong[karāhah (makrūh)] of not
  following the sunnah.

#strong[Second opinion (within Ḥanafī school, also the Shāfiʿī
position):] The round that occurs before reaching Ḥajar al-Aswad is
#strong[not counted]. The ṭawāf effectively begins only when the pilgrim
reaches Ḥajar al-Aswad, and any circling before that point does not
count toward the seven rounds. This is #strong[not the preferred (rājiḥ)
Ḥanafī opinion].

==== Historical background
<historical-background>
When #strong[Ibrāhīm عليه السلام] completed the construction of the
Ka'bah, he asked his son #strong[Ismāʿīl عليه السلام] to bring him a
stone to #strong[mark] the starting point of ṭawāf. Ismāʿīl brought
stones, but eventually #strong[Jibrīl عليه السلام] brought the stone
from #strong[Jannah] --- the #strong[Ḥajar al-Aswad]. Ibrāhīm said:
#emph["I have no further need --- Allāh has fulfilled it."] Both parties
use this narration --- one takes it as proof of sunnah, the other as
proof of obligation.

#horizontalrule

=== Wājib 3 --- Circling from the right side (الطواف من جانب اليمين)
<wajib-3-circling-from-the-right-side>
#strong[Ruling (Ḥanafī): Wājib] --- the pilgrim must circle the Ka'bah
keeping it to his #strong[left] (i.e.~moving #strong[counter-clockwise],
starting to the #strong[right] of Ḥajar al-Aswad).

#strong[Ruling (Shāfiʿī): Farḍ (shart)] --- ṭawāf is #strong[invalid] if
done in the wrong direction.

#figure(
  align(center)[#table(
    columns: (22.73%, 77.27%),
    align: (auto,auto,),
    table.header([Position], [If ṭawāf done in wrong direction],),
